## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-tenant ordering, handle late arrivals, and preserve at-least-once delivery with idempotence in a KCNA-like streaming system.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant sequencing and a central Sequencer
- Idempotent downstream sink with deduplication
- Watermark-based late-arrival handling
- Cross-tenant isolation and scalable design
- Testing strategies: chaos, delays, churn

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sketch of central Sequencer + dedupe logic
class Sequencer {
  constructor() { this.counters = new Map(); }
  next(tenantId) {
    const prev = this.counters.get(tenantId) || 0;
    const next = prev + 1;
    this.counters.set(tenantId, next);
    return next;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evolve to thousands of tenants with a federated sequencer?
- How would you verify correctness under clock skew and message replays?

## Why This Is Asked

To test understanding of consumer rebalances and offset management, a common pitfall for beginners where duplicates occur if offsets aren't tracked during rebalances.

## Key Concepts

- Consumer group rebalancing
- Per-topic offset tracking
- In-flight state
- Safe commit on revoke
- Timeouts and fault tolerance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo code showing API hooks
class RebalanceSafeConsumer {
  constructor() { this.committedOffsets = new Map(); this.inFlight = new Set(); }
  onPartitionsAssigned(parts) { for (const p of parts) { const k = `${p.topic}:${p.partition}`; const start = (this.committedOffsets.get(k) ?? -1) + 1; this.seek(p.topic, p.partition, start); } }
  onPartitionsRevoked(parts) { const revoked = new Set(parts.map(p => `${p.topic}:${p.partition}`)); /* wait for inFlight on revoked before committing */ }
  processMessage(msg) { const id = `${msg.topic}:${msg.partition}:${msg.offset}`; this.inFlight.add(id); // downstream write then:
    this.inFlight.delete(id); const key = `${msg.topic}:${msg.partition}`; this.committedOffsets.set(key, msg.offset); }
  commitOffsets() { /* commit logic here */ }
}
```

Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design policy-driven routing, multi-tenant isolation, and exactly-once-like guarantees in KCNA.

Key Concepts

- Policy registry: per-tenant sensitivity rules
- Per-tenant ordering: stable keying by tenantId|sequence
- Routing targets: events_public, events_internal, events_restricted
- Violation handling: quarantine_topic and DLQ
- Idempotent sinks: dedupe by (tenantId, eventId)

Code Example

```javascript
// Route a raw event according to tenant policy
function routeEvent(event, policyStore) {
  const { tenantId, sensitivity } = event.headers;
  const policy = policyStore.lookup(tenantId);
  if (!policy.allows(sensitivity)) {
    deliver('quarantine_topic', event, { reason: 'policy_violation' });
    return { routed: false, reason: 'policy_violation' };
  }
  const target = policy.targetTopicFor(sensitivity);
  const key = `${tenantId}|${event.headers.sequence}`;
  deliver(target, event, { key });
  return { routed: true, target };
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you test per-tenant ordering guarantees across regional KCNA clusters?
- How do you handle policy changes mid-stream without violating ordering or DLQ semantics?
- What metrics and alerting would you add for policy violations and routing failures?

